<div class="wrap-narrow">
  <header class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">Limits</span>
    <h1>What eui-score does not engrave</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Anything outside the subset is refused, with the character where the engraver stopped. The messages below
      are not copied in: each source is handed to the engraver when this page is shown, and its answer is what
      gets printed.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <% for refusal in refusals %>
      <div class="refusal">
        <h3><%= refusal["what"] %></h3>
        <pre><code><%- score_highlight(refusal["src"]) %></code></pre>
        <% if refusal["message"].nil? %>
          <p class="muted">Accepted by this version of the engraver.</p>
        <% else %>
          <p class="score-error"><%= refusal["message"] %></p>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <h2>No longer limits</h2>
    <p>
      Since EUI 0.11 (Soli 2.28.2), the engraver reads LilyPond as people write it: variables and
      <code>\global</code>, <code>\new Voice</code> with <code>\voiceOne</code>/<code>\voiceTwo</code>,
      <code>\new Lyrics \lyricsto</code>, <code>\language</code>, <code>\fixed</code>, <code>\transpose</code>,
      <code>q</code>, <code>\partial</code>, <code>\cadenzaOn</code>, spacers, <code>\repeat unfold</code> and
      <code>percent</code>, grace notes, the tenor, soprano, baritone and <code>treble_8</code> clefs, fingerings,
      <code>\markup</code>, <code>\mark</code>, <code>\ottava</code>, phrasing slurs, <code>\trill</code> and other
      ornaments, <code>\arpeggio</code>, <code>\glissando</code>, tremolos, and <code>\new ChordNames</code>,
      <code>\new DrumStaff</code> and <code>\new TabStaff</code> — each drawn as LilyPond 2.25 draws it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since Soli 2.28.4, the server engraves with the same engraver as the browser: 64th and 128th notes, a
      tuplet inside a tuplet, up to four voices on a staff, several <code>\addlyrics</code> verses, any metre;
      a note longer than what is left of its bar is cut at the bar line into tied notes, as LilyPond's
      <code>Completion_heads_engraver</code> cuts it; the sustain pedal (<code>\sustainOn</code>,
      <code>\sustainOff</code>) is drawn and held; and LilyPond 2.24's jumps, <code>\jump "D.S. al Coda"</code>
      and <code>\fine</code>, are set against their bar line. The library, the shared pages and the PDFs show
      what the editor shows.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since Soli 2.28.5, real LilyPond files open. The layout settings nearly every file writes —
      <code>\override</code>, <code>\set</code>, <code>\tweak</code>, a <code>\with { … }</code> or a
      <code>\layout</code> block, header fields no title prints — are read and passed over, each named in a
      warning, so the music after them is engraved; <code>\stemUp</code>/<code>\stemDown</code> and
      <code>\autoBeamOff</code> are honoured. <code>\breathe</code>, <code>\caesura</code>, <code>\upbow</code>,
      <code>\downbow</code>, <code>\flageolet</code>, <code>\espressivo</code>, <code>\laissezVibrer</code>,
      <code>\repeatTie</code>, <code>\cresc</code> and <code>\dim</code> in words and text spans are drawn, and
      so are forced and cautionary accidentals (<code>cis!</code>, <code>cis?</code>). Several
      <code>\score</code> blocks, or a <code>\book</code> of <code>\bookpart</code>s, become movements, each
      starting on a line of its own. A clef or a key may change in the middle of a bar, and
      <code>\compressMMRests</code> gathers bars of rest into one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since Soli 2.28.9, the piano's two staves are crossed as LilyPond crosses them: a beam over notes of both
      staves is drawn once between them, slurs and ties go from one staff to the other, <code>\autoChange</code>
      shares one voice between the hands, and <code>\showStaffSwitch</code> and <code>\crossStaff</code> are drawn.
      The common <code>\override</code>, <code>\set</code>, <code>\tweak</code>, <code>\omit</code> and
      <code>\hide</code> settings are followed instead of passed over (directions, offsets, sizes, beam grouping,
      mark and time-signature styles, note-head styles, bar numbers). <code>\RemoveEmptyStaves</code> hides a
      staff that rests for a whole system, and <code>\partCombine</code> sets two parts on one staff with
      <em>a2</em>, <em>Solo</em> and two voices. Lines are broken over the whole piece, and the
      <code>\paper</code> settings for widths, indents, ragged lines and pages, the space between systems and the
      number of systems or pages are honoured.
    </p>
    <p>
      The score editor now edits what the engraver draws in such files instead of leaving them to the text:
      staves changing key or metre apart, a tempo, a mark, a bar line or a repeat sign inside a bar,
      <code>\cadenzaOn</code>, a tuplet inside a tuplet, every clef the engraver draws, <code>\open</code> and
      <code>\stopped</code>, <code>\tiny</code> and <code>\small</code>, <code>\new TabStaff</code>,
      <code>\new CueVoice</code> and <code>\cueDuring</code>, and an ossia. <code>\override</code>,
      <code>\set</code>, <code>\tweak</code>, a staff's <code>\with</code>, <code>\layout</code> and
      <code>\paper</code> are kept where they are written, and a staff made by <code>\partCombine</code> or
      <code>\autoChange</code> is kept as written, its notes edited in the text.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Layout</strong>: vertical spacing does not tighten on a crowded page, a beam across the staves
        is sloped more simply than LilyPond's, and the accidentals of a note moved to the other staff are still
        counted in its own staff's bar.</li>
      <li><strong>Settings</strong>: an <code>\override</code> not in the list above, and smaller staves
        (<code>StaffSymbol.staff-space</code>) for an ossia or cue notes, are still passed over with a
        warning.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">
      Not sure? Paste your source into the <a href="<%= playground_path() %>">playground</a>: the engraver's
      message points at the exact character where it stops.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
